WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:02.802
-Matisse, thank you so much
for coming on the show.

00:00:02.802 --> 00:00:04.804
Nice to meet you through Zoom,

00:00:04.804 --> 00:00:08.474
but I'm a big fan of you,
but I'm also a fan

00:00:08.474 --> 00:00:10.843
of what you've been doing
while you're in the bubble.

00:00:10.843 --> 00:00:15.514
I want to go back,
if you don't mind, to March.

00:00:15.514 --> 00:00:18.485
NBA suspends the season.

00:00:18.485 --> 00:00:20.052
Where are you,
what goes through your mind,

00:00:20.052 --> 00:00:22.121
and what were you
thinking about?

00:00:22.121 --> 00:00:25.091
-Yeah, it was --
It's crazy 'cause, for me,

00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:26.959
like, just being in the NBA
is crazy.

00:00:26.959 --> 00:00:28.661
I'm just trying
to figure it out.

00:00:28.661 --> 00:00:31.730
And then as I think
I'm hitting, like, a groove

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:35.034
and finding a routine,
like, everything is shut down.

00:00:35.034 --> 00:00:38.804
And to have this be my life
and kind of my new normal

00:00:38.804 --> 00:00:40.472
and then just to be put on hold

00:00:40.472 --> 00:00:43.243
in a way that no one's
ever seen before is confusing.

00:00:43.243 --> 00:00:46.945
And it left me and a lot of guys
in this limbo

00:00:46.945 --> 00:00:49.616
of just trying to figure out
what's going to happen,

00:00:49.616 --> 00:00:52.518
what's coming next,
or what do we do now.

00:00:52.518 --> 00:00:54.787
-Yeah.
-It's been -- It was weird.

00:00:54.787 --> 00:00:58.257
-What did you think first
when you heard about the bubble?

00:00:58.257 --> 00:01:00.893
-The --
I mean, it sounded crazy.

00:01:00.893 --> 00:01:03.663
I mean, it is crazy, like just
the fact that we're even here.

00:01:03.663 --> 00:01:05.398
But when I first heard it,
I'm like,

00:01:05.398 --> 00:01:07.333
"Well, there's no way
they can do that, right?"

00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:08.701
-And then what -- what sold you?

00:01:08.701 --> 00:01:11.771
Like, "Okay, they really
have thought this out."

00:01:11.771 --> 00:01:12.872
Was there a moment?

00:01:12.872 --> 00:01:15.442
-Yeah. For me,
it was a couple things.

00:01:15.442 --> 00:01:18.311
So, we started -- we started
doing workouts back in Philly

00:01:18.311 --> 00:01:20.647
with our --
at our team practice facility.

00:01:20.647 --> 00:01:22.949
And that was, like,
a scaled-down version

00:01:22.949 --> 00:01:24.417
of kind of
what we're doing here.

00:01:24.417 --> 00:01:26.185
And I felt pretty good
about that.

00:01:26.185 --> 00:01:29.589
And then they also sent out
this manual, I guess,

00:01:29.589 --> 00:01:31.624
like, this -- of what
they're going to be doing.

00:01:31.624 --> 00:01:34.059
It was like over a hundred pages

00:01:34.059 --> 00:01:36.296
of the health protocols
and everything.

00:01:36.296 --> 00:01:38.031
And I was like --
I mean, I didn't read it,

00:01:38.031 --> 00:01:39.965
but I definitely saw
that if they're going to put

00:01:39.965 --> 00:01:42.101
that much into...
to send to players,

00:01:42.101 --> 00:01:44.703
I was like, "Okay,
they definitely put some..."

00:01:44.703 --> 00:01:46.339
-It's almost like
the Apple terms and conditions.

00:01:46.339 --> 00:01:48.007
You're like, "Yeah, I accept."

00:01:48.007 --> 00:01:49.575
-Yeah. [ Laughs ]
-"I'm not gonna read it."

00:01:49.575 --> 00:01:51.010
Yeah. Please.

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:53.646
Well, the reason
why I wanted to have you on --

00:01:53.646 --> 00:01:56.048
because you've been
documenting everything

00:01:56.048 --> 00:01:57.283
and putting it online.

00:01:57.283 --> 00:01:58.884
I think it's brilliant.

00:01:58.884 --> 00:02:02.521
And you're filming everything
and editing it yourself,

00:02:02.521 --> 00:02:04.423
and it sounds and looks great.

00:02:04.423 --> 00:02:05.691
Have you done this before?

00:02:05.691 --> 00:02:07.626
Are you --
Have you made videos?

00:02:07.626 --> 00:02:09.762
-Kind of.
My friends and I, like --

00:02:09.762 --> 00:02:11.464
You know,
you go on a little road trip

00:02:11.464 --> 00:02:13.533
or you go hang out or something.

00:02:13.533 --> 00:02:15.602
I would shoot some stuff,
but it was never something

00:02:15.602 --> 00:02:17.503
I'd put out
because, I mean, as you know,

00:02:17.503 --> 00:02:19.405
it's kind of hard to put
yourself out there like this.

00:02:19.405 --> 00:02:22.341
And for me, like, having not --

00:02:22.341 --> 00:02:24.176
I mean, in college
and in high school,

00:02:24.176 --> 00:02:26.612
having not really been
a big -- a well-known person

00:02:26.612 --> 00:02:28.648
or, like, a well-known
basketball player,

00:02:28.648 --> 00:02:30.783
that was still
very, very weird to me.

00:02:30.783 --> 00:02:33.619
And I would make these videos
we'd share amongst each other,

00:02:33.619 --> 00:02:36.322
and then that was kind of it,
but now that I'm here --

00:02:36.322 --> 00:02:37.890
And, like,
right before we left,

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:40.159
I was going back and forth
with my friends like,

00:02:40.159 --> 00:02:43.562
"This is an amazing opportunity,
a historical moment,

00:02:43.562 --> 00:02:45.899
and I want to capture it."

00:02:45.899 --> 00:02:47.534
And I know what to do
because, like,

00:02:47.534 --> 00:02:50.737
we've been doing this, we've
messing around with it before.

00:02:50.737 --> 00:02:53.239
And, yeah, so we went
back and forth a lot

00:02:53.239 --> 00:02:54.473
and then, you know,
"Just do it."

00:02:54.473 --> 00:02:55.775
I was like,
"Yeah, might as well."

00:02:55.775 --> 00:02:57.410
Like, get out of my comfort zone
a little bit,

00:02:57.410 --> 00:03:00.012
put myself out there, and
hopefully some people enjoy it.

00:03:00.012 --> 00:03:01.480
-Good for you,
'cause it's worked.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:04.750
I also saw that
you solved a Rubik's Cube

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:07.219
in like, I don't know,
90 seconds

00:03:07.219 --> 00:03:10.923
or some crazy thing that
I've seen before, like, online.

00:03:10.923 --> 00:03:12.591
But how did you learn
how to do that?

00:03:12.591 --> 00:03:15.061
Was that something you used to
do in high school or...?

00:03:15.061 --> 00:03:17.397
-I learned because in --
when I was in college,

00:03:17.397 --> 00:03:20.599
it was either, like,
my sophomore or junior year.

00:03:20.599 --> 00:03:22.936
I was at the gym over the summer
with my dad,

00:03:22.936 --> 00:03:24.604
and there was this little kid
playing with a Rubik's Cube.

00:03:24.604 --> 00:03:26.606
And I went up to him.
He was probably like 12.

00:03:26.606 --> 00:03:28.875
I was like, "There's no way
you can solve this thing."

00:03:28.875 --> 00:03:31.344
And he seemed like a little kid.
And he's like,

00:03:31.344 --> 00:03:33.445
"Oh, I can. I can. I swear.
Get out your timer."

00:03:33.445 --> 00:03:35.048
Said, "Okay, here we go."

00:03:35.048 --> 00:03:36.749
So, I hit start,

00:03:36.749 --> 00:03:39.085
and this kid just starts
going crazy with the thing.

00:03:39.085 --> 00:03:41.320
And in 35 seconds,
he had solved it.

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:43.890
I was like, "Wow. Okay."

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:45.758
Like, it was
really, really cool.

00:03:45.758 --> 00:03:49.729
And for me, I'm like,
"If this, like, 12-year-old kid

00:03:49.729 --> 00:03:52.465
can figure out how to solve it
in 30 seconds..."

00:03:52.465 --> 00:03:55.034
I -- At the time,
I was probably 21.

00:03:55.034 --> 00:03:58.838
I'm like, "I can at least
get it done to some degree.

00:03:58.838 --> 00:04:00.272
I don't care
how long it takes."

00:04:00.272 --> 00:04:03.276
So, like, after that...
wanted a Rubik's Cube

00:04:03.276 --> 00:04:05.544
and then just, like,
sat on YouTube and studied,

00:04:05.544 --> 00:04:07.680
and now it's just, like,
a party trick, I guess.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:11.250
-Also, if people
watching our show

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:13.452
have not seen your videos,
they should.

00:04:13.452 --> 00:04:14.854
They're so interesting.

00:04:14.854 --> 00:04:17.557
But one thing is that
you didn't really hold back

00:04:17.557 --> 00:04:21.527
on conversations
you're having with your team,

00:04:21.527 --> 00:04:23.195
even about racial inequality

00:04:23.195 --> 00:04:26.699
and, like,
important conversations.

00:04:26.699 --> 00:04:28.701
Were you nervous
to put that out there

00:04:28.701 --> 00:04:31.137
or talk about that
or document that?

00:04:31.137 --> 00:04:33.072
-Yeah, I mean, I was --

00:04:33.072 --> 00:04:34.806
For all of it,
for documenting it,

00:04:34.806 --> 00:04:37.777
just 'cause a lot of it
was the opinion of other people,

00:04:37.777 --> 00:04:40.145
and, like, that's a very
intimate thing to share

00:04:40.145 --> 00:04:41.714
on someone else's behalf.

00:04:41.714 --> 00:04:45.384
So, for me, it was --
it was nerve-racking to document

00:04:45.384 --> 00:04:47.753
and also just to --
Through editing it,

00:04:47.753 --> 00:04:50.423
I wanted to put these guys
who are being vulnerable

00:04:50.423 --> 00:04:52.792
and open and sharing
and discussing these topics

00:04:52.792 --> 00:04:54.560
in the best light possible.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:57.029
And, I mean,
I hope I did them justice,

00:04:57.029 --> 00:04:58.631
but I was really, really happy

00:04:58.631 --> 00:05:00.199
that they were willing
to share that with me

00:05:00.199 --> 00:05:02.201
so I could share it
with, I mean, the world.

00:05:02.201 --> 00:05:03.469
-It was great they did that,

00:05:03.469 --> 00:05:05.137
and it keeps
the conversation going.

00:05:05.137 --> 00:05:06.773
And, you know --
And I just think

00:05:06.773 --> 00:05:08.540
it was really cool
that you did that.

00:05:08.540 --> 00:05:12.578
And I saw that the players
get to choose different words

00:05:12.578 --> 00:05:17.283
or phrases on their jersey,
and you chose "vote."

00:05:17.283 --> 00:05:18.484
-Yeah.

00:05:18.484 --> 00:05:20.419
-Why is that important to you?

00:05:20.419 --> 00:05:22.055
-Yeah, that's -- that's one
that is very --

00:05:22.055 --> 00:05:24.057
hits very close to home
just 'cause

00:05:24.057 --> 00:05:26.058
it's really important
to my family.

00:05:26.058 --> 00:05:29.328
And my dad --
my dad grew up in Haiti,

00:05:29.328 --> 00:05:30.596
so essentially he's --

00:05:30.596 --> 00:05:32.231
To grow up
with immigrant parents,

00:05:32.231 --> 00:05:35.200
like, they have
a different perspective

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:38.537
because they come from somewhere
outside of the US,

00:05:38.537 --> 00:05:42.174
where we at times take a lot of
what we have here for granted.

00:05:42.174 --> 00:05:45.578
And so for him to come from
somewhere where their leader

00:05:45.578 --> 00:05:48.413
was a dictator,
not a president,

00:05:48.413 --> 00:05:52.017
he really -- my dad
puts a lot of value on the vote

00:05:52.017 --> 00:05:55.054
'cause he knows what it's like
to not have a voice.

00:05:55.054 --> 00:05:56.489
And to live in a country

00:05:56.489 --> 00:05:58.791
where we have the opportunity
to have a voice and be heard

00:05:58.791 --> 00:06:01.126
and make change with it is --

00:06:01.126 --> 00:06:02.595
I mean, it's an opportunity
that you really --

00:06:02.595 --> 00:06:03.796
you can't take for granted.

00:06:03.796 --> 00:06:05.297
And I think
that it's been amazing

00:06:05.297 --> 00:06:07.533
that I have the opportunity
as a basketball player

00:06:07.533 --> 00:06:09.569
to spread this message
on my jersey,

00:06:09.569 --> 00:06:10.769
through my videos, with you,

00:06:10.769 --> 00:06:13.739
and then also just
that we have as a country,

00:06:13.739 --> 00:06:15.474
and even with my generation,

00:06:15.474 --> 00:06:17.910
are making a huge effort
to make it a thing

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:20.446
that we're excited about
and passionate about

00:06:20.446 --> 00:06:23.082
and take seriously
because it is something serious

00:06:23.082 --> 00:06:24.583
and it isn't something
we should take for granted.

00:06:24.583 --> 00:06:26.752
And I hope that
in this next election

00:06:26.752 --> 00:06:28.888
that we have
a much better turnout

00:06:28.888 --> 00:06:30.923
than we did four years ago.

00:06:30.923 --> 00:06:33.760
-I think we will.
I know you have a game tonight.

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:36.662
Matisse, go out there and make
more history tonight, buddy.

00:06:36.662 --> 00:06:38.731
Thank you so much
for doing our show.

00:06:38.731 --> 00:06:40.533
I appreciate it.
-Thank you for having me.

00:06:40.533 --> 00:06:43.101
-Matisse Thybulle, everybody.
Check out Matisse's YouTube page

00:06:43.101 --> 00:06:45.738
for more episodes of
"Welcome to the Bubble."

